## Node topology

Omitting `node` blocks entirely creates the kind default: one `control-plane`
node and one `worker`. Declaring any `node` block replaces that default, so a
configuration with a single `worker` block and no `control-plane` is invalid and
kind will reject it.

Declaring more than one `control-plane` node produces a highly available
cluster; kind adds an external load balancer container in front of them
automatically.

## Waiting for readiness

`wait_for_ready` bounds how long the provider waits, in seconds. It covers two
distinct phases:

1. kind's own wait for the control plane to respond.
2. The provider's wait for every node to report `Ready`, performed only when
   `wait_for_nodes_ready` is `true` (the default).

Set `wait_for_nodes_ready = false` when installing a CNI yourself with
`disable_default_cni = true` — nodes stay `NotReady` until that CNI is running,
which cannot happen until after `terraform apply` returns.

